These still raise: `float(10**400)` and `int(float("inf"))` throw `OverflowError`, and `float(obj)` runs arbitrary `__float__`. Could we check types up front instead of catching, so the code can't throw (same idea as `_coerce_float` in google-genai `interactions.py`)?

```suggestion
def _as_float(value: Any) -> float | None:
    if isinstance(value, float):
        return value
    if (
        isinstance(value, int)
        and not isinstance(value, bool)
        and abs(value) <= sys.float_info.max
    ):
        return float(value)
    return None


def _as_int(value: Any) -> int | None:
    if isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool):
        return value
    return None
```

(needs `import sys`; a test case with `temperature=10**400` would cover it.)
